    "evidence_text": "Introduction\tDecoding\n\nEEG is non-invasive, portable, low cost, and provides satisfactory temporal resolution.\n\nThis makes EEG suitable to realize BCI systems.\n\nEEG data, however, is\n\nof accuracy over the baseline.\n\nThe few exceptions include  ,  .\n\nWe now turn to describing our proposed models.\n\nWe formulate the problem of categorizing EEG data based\n\nJoint variability of electrodes\tMultichannel EEG data is high dimensional multivariate time series data whose dimensionality depends on the number of electrodes.\n\nIt is a major hurdle to optimally encode information from these EEG data into lower dimensional space.\n\nBesides, we found that instead of using the raw multi-channel high-dimensional EEG requiring large training times and resource requirements, it is\n\nadvantageous to first reduce its dimensionality by capturing the information transfer among the electrodes.\n\nWe present our sample 2-D EEG cross-covariance matrices (of two individuals) in Fig. FIGREF2 .\n\nas 4 words(pat, pot, knew and gnaw).\n\nThe dataset consists of 14 participants, with each prompt presented 11 times to each individual.\n\nTraining and hyperparameter selection\tWe performed two sets of experiments with the single-trial EEG data.",
